  :adr/body "# Full public-reference genetics into rasen, with kotobase.net as the query face

## The question that started it

\"Is all the human DNA data in kotobase.net?\" No, and three separate layers were
in the way. Measuring each of them, rather than answering from the shape of the
repo, is what produced this decision.

**Layer 1 — rasen does not write to kotobase.net at all.** `grep -rn kotobase`
over `src/`, `tools/` and the EDN roots of `etzhayyim/actor-rasen` returns zero
hits. The file named `kotoba.cljc` is a local append-only ledger writer that
states \"performs NO network I/O\"; publication goes to IPFS pin + IPNS. The
kotobase.net tenant plane was never wired as a destination.

**Layer 2 — the corpus itself was 53 nodes.** Measured in
`data/seed-genome-graph.kotoba.edn`: 16 gene, 14 phenotype, 12 variant, 6
population, 5 pathway; the persisted ledger carries `:tx/count 583`.

**Layer 3 — the path that would grow it does not run.** `ingest.cljc` refuses
live ingest (\"REFUSED: live gene ingest (MyGene.info / MyVariant.info) is
G7-gated network + not ported\"); only `--offline` re-addressing works. The
Python original it was ported from is not in the repository. Upstream is fine:
mygene.info and myvariant.info both answer 200. What is missing is one unported
path, not egress and not the sources.

## Decision

Ingest the **full public reference corpus** — ClinVar in its entirety, gnomAD
population-aggregate allele frequencies — and keep **individual genotypes out**,
which is not a scope cut but the actor's constitution (G1: no individual
genotype registry, no identifiable personal sequence, no sample- or family-level
data; population frequencies at super-population scale only; location as COARSE
cytoband only).

Destination is **both faces, with an explicit direction**: the append-only
content-addressed ledger stays canonical, and kotobase.net carries the same
datoms as a **projection** — deletable and rebuildable from the ledger, per the
superproject's projection rule. kotobase.net is where Datalog reaches the graph;
it is never where the graph lives.

## What the scale actually is (measured, not estimated from reputation)

| source | measured |
|---|---|
| ClinVar `variant_summary.txt.gz` | 442,495,433 bytes (2026-08-31) |
| ClinVar GRCh38 VCF | 193,427,450 bytes |
| gnomAD v4.1 genomes, **chr21 alone** | 7,758,981,978 bytes |
| gnomAD v4.1 exomes, **chr21 alone** | 2,250,319,809 bytes |

chr21 is among the smallest chromosomes. The full gnomAD sites release is
hundreds of gigabytes. That settles a design question rather than describing
one: **the raw VCFs are not ingested.** Only per-variant super-population
allele frequencies are extracted, which is also all G1 permits. Any raw bulk
that must be retained goes to B2 + DataLad, never into git.

Normalising 5,282 real ClinVar lines through the new code: 2,619 GRCh38 rows
kept, 2,663 skipped, 4,365 nodes and 9,076 edges, 0 G1 violations. Kept plus
skipped equals rows read. Extrapolated to the full release that is roughly 5.8M
lines, about 2.9M GRCh38 rows, on the order of 10M edges and tens of millions of
datoms.

**Consequence the measurement forces:** the ledger is one EDN file holding 583
datoms today. It cannot hold that. Sharding of the ledger is a prerequisite for
the full run, not an optimisation of it.

## Landed in this step

`rasen.methods.clinvar` (etzhayyim/actor-rasen main a261f970) — the full-corpus
normaliser, emitting the same node and edge shapes as the bounded producer so
the two are interchangeable downstream.

G1 is enforced as a check rather than described in a comment. `variant_summary`
carries Chromosome / Start / Stop / PositionVCF; the ontology permits coarse
cytoband only. `g1-violations` refuses any node carrying a coordinate attr,
`row->graph` runs it over everything it emits, and it throws rather than
reporting clean when handed a non-map, so an unreadable node is UNVERIFIED and
not silently clean. The negative test pins the refusal reason, so an unrelated
failure cannot be counted as a successful discrimination.

The suite caught one real defect: MONDO ids were built with a doubled prefix
(`ph.mondo-mondo-0011450`), which would have split every MONDO condition into
two node ids — one from this producer, one from `ingest` — and the split would
have been invisible in both. MONDO values carry their own prefix.

Skipped rows return nil rather than an empty graph, and the accumulator reports
rows and skips, so coverage cannot be silently overstated.

## Sharding — landed (rasen main a979d183)

`rasen.methods.ledger-shards`. Sharding does not touch the chain: a tx cid is
sha256 over (datoms, prev-cid) and nothing else, so splitting the same linear
sequence across files preserves every cid. `migrate!` asserts that instead of
assuming it; on the real on-disk ledger (1 tx, 583 datoms) the head comes
through byte-identical as b17a2fa1…d99990.

The failure worth engineering against is not splitting but INCOMPLETENESS. Once
the log is many files, walking the shards you can find yields a shorter chain
that verifies perfectly — \"could not read shard 7\" returns the same ok as \"read
every shard and all linked\". The index is therefore the evidence floor:
`verify!` reports ok only after seeing exactly as many txs as the index says
exist, and it separates :no-index, :index-invalid, :shard-missing,
:chain-broken and :count-mismatch instead of collapsing them into false.
`index-violations` throws for a non-index rather than reporting no violations.
`reindex!` refuses to synthesise an index for an empty directory. `append-txs!`
refuses a run that does not continue the head.

Both refusal paths were broken deliberately and only their own tests failed:
skipping unreadable shards failed the missing-shard tests, and removing the
count floor failed the truncation test alone.

**A defect in the existing ledger, found by trying to migrate it.**
`kotoba/verify-chain` returned `{:ok true :length 0}` for a path that does not
exist. `read-log` returning [] for a missing file is correct — `autorun` starts
a fresh chain from genesis on a machine with no ledger — but folding that []
into verification made \"there is no ledger here\" indistinguishable from \"I
walked the whole ledger and every link holds\". It now refuses with :no-log.
This is the same class the superproject CLAUDE.md records, sitting in the
ledger's own verifier.

Two writing hazards worth carrying forward. `pr-str` cannot write the index: it
collapses same-namespace maps to `#:index{…}`, which the minimal reader in
`kotoba.cljc` parses to the string \"#:index\" — silently, yielding an index that
is not a map. And `data/persisted/` is gitignored by design (machine-local,
regenerable from the committed seed), so a worktree has no ledger at all; that
absence is exactly what surfaced the verifier defect.

The actual migration of the live ledger is deliberately NOT done here. It
belongs with the streaming runner — migrating with nothing to append is churn,
and `publish.cljc` / `datom-emit` still read the single file.

## Not done, and blocking

1. **The G7 streaming runner.** Nothing yet streams the 442 MB gzip; the
   normaliser is pure and takes parsed lines.
2. **The kotobase.net publisher.** `kotoba-lang/kotobase-client` is the canonical
   CACAO-authed client and `net-kotobase/commoncrawler` is the existing precedent
   for bulk EAVT into kotobase.net through the `kotobase.store/IStore` seam.
   Neither is wired to rasen yet. Do not write a third client.
3. **gnomAD AF extraction.** Per-variant super-population frequencies only.

## Notes for whoever continues

The ADR number in the source docstrings of `clinvar.cljc` and
`test_clinvar.cljc` briefly said 2609061800, which belongs to a different ADR
(kotoba-web-architecture-fit). Corrected in rasen main 16d55c50.

While landing this, a superproject EDN document written by another loop
(`90-docs/business/revenue-agent-loop/runs/0037-kotobase-stab-001.edn`) was
found broken by four raw quotes inside a body string, which closed it early and
left the remainder to be read as symbols. Escaping restored it; content
unchanged. This is the failure mode the superproject CLAUDE.md already records,
and it recurred in a document written the same day."}]
